From: Altium-on-Wine kit
Subject: server: Do not kill a thread from send_thread_wakeup() on EPIPE

Modules: server
Base: wine-11.16 (also applies to wine-staging 11.16 and upstream master)

wake_up() walks an object's wait queue and calls wake_thread() for each waiter. When the
waiter's process has already died, send_thread_wakeup() got EPIPE and called kill_thread()
right there. For the last thread of a process that destroys its handle table, which can free
the object wake_up() is iterating (e.g. the internal sync of the dead thread's message queue,
signaled by a message another process posted). wake_up() then reads freed memory and the
server crashes, freezing every other process in the prefix. AddressSanitizer:
  heap-use-after-free thread.c:1355 in wake_up, freed by release_object <- handle_table_destroy
  <- remove_process_thread <- kill_thread <- send_thread_wakeup <- wake_thread <- wake_up.

On EPIPE just report the failure; the dead client's closed request fd gets the thread killed
from the main loop moments later, outside any wait queue walk.

--- a/server/thread.c
+++ b/server/thread.c
@@ -1179,7 +1179,13 @@
     if (ret >= 0)
         fatal_protocol_error( thread, "partial wakeup write %d\n", ret );
     else if (errno == EPIPE)
-        kill_thread( thread, 0 );  /* normal death */
+    {
+        /* Normal death: the client is gone and its closed request fd will get the thread
+         * killed from the main loop. Don't kill it here: we can be called from wake_up()
+         * while it iterates an object's wait queue, and killing the last thread of a process
+         * destroys its handle table, which can free that very object (e.g. the internal sync
+         * of the dead thread's message queue) and crash the server. */
+    }
     else
         fatal_protocol_error( thread, "write: %s\n", strerror( errno ));
     return -1;
